---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/forty-thieves/', label: 'Play Forty Thieves' }}
>
  <p>
    Forty Thieves is the patience game for people who want a real fight. Two full decks, strict
    same-suit building, and single-card moves combine to make it one of the toughest solitaires in
    common play — which is exactly why it has so many devoted fans. This page is a complete
    reference to the rules: the deal, the objective, the legal moves, the stock, and how you win. If
    you'd rather learn at the table, you can <a href="/forty-thieves/">play Forty Thieves</a> right
    away.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Forty Thieves?</h2>
  <p>
    Forty Thieves is a solitaire game played with <strong>two standard decks shuffled together</strong>
    — 104 cards in all. It takes its name from the forty cards dealt face up into the tableau at the
    start. The table has three areas: ten <strong>tableau columns</strong> where you do your
    untangling, a face-down <strong>stock</strong> with its face-up <strong>waste</strong> pile, and
    eight <strong>foundations</strong> where the winning piles are built. The double deck and its
    strict rules give the game a reputation for being unforgiving, and clearing one is genuinely
    satisfying.
  </p>

  <h2>The deal</h2>
  <p>
    Shuffle the two decks together and deal <strong>ten tableau columns of four cards each, all face
    up</strong>. That is 10&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;4 = <strong>40 cards</strong> on the table, which is
    where the name comes from. The remaining <strong>64 cards form the stock</strong>, placed face
    down to one side. The eight foundations all start empty. Because every tableau card is face up,
    you can read the whole layout from the first move — the only hidden cards are the ones still
    waiting in the stock.
  </p>

  <h2>The objective</h2>
  <p>
    You win by building all eight <strong>foundations</strong> up by suit, each running from
    <strong>Ace to King</strong>. Because you are playing two decks, there are eight foundations —
    <strong>two for each suit</strong> — and each fills with thirteen cards. When every foundation
    holds a complete Ace-to-King suit, all <strong>104 cards</strong> are home and the game is won.
  </p>

  <h2>Legal moves</h2>
  <p>
    Forty Thieves has a short, strict set of rules. The two that define the game are same-suit
    building and single-card moves.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Tableau builds down in suit.</strong> You may place a card on a tableau column only if
      it is the <em>same suit</em> as the top card and exactly one rank lower — a 9&spades; goes
      only on a 10&spades;, never on a 10 of another suit. This is far more restrictive than the
      alternating-colour building you may know from other games.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You move one card at a time.</strong> There is no group or sequence move. To shift a
      run you relocate its cards one by one, using other columns and empty spaces as relays and
      rebuilding the run where you want it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Foundations build up by suit from the Ace.</strong> A card goes to a foundation only
      if it is an Ace starting an empty foundation, or the next rank up in the same suit as that
      foundation's top card.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An empty column accepts any single card.</strong> When a tableau column is cleared,
      you may place any one card there — a precious resource, since empty columns are your main tool
      for shuffling cards around.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The stock and waste</h2>
  <p>
    When you run out of useful tableau moves, you turn the <strong>stock</strong> one card at a time
    onto a face-up <strong>waste</strong> pile. The <strong>top card of the waste is always
    available</strong> to play to a foundation or onto a tableau column, but the cards beneath it
    are locked until you deal past them. Crucially, there is <strong>no redeal</strong>: once the
    stock is used up, it is gone for good. Every flip is a one-way decision, so think before you
    dig — a card buried in the waste, or one you fail to use when it surfaces, may never come back.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Forty Thieves is hard</h2>
  <p>
    Three rules stack against you. <strong>Same-suit building</strong> means a 9&spades; will only
    sit on a 10&spades; — your options at any moment are narrow. <strong>Single-card moves</strong>
    mean even a short run takes several careful steps and several free spaces to relocate. And the
    <strong>no-redeal stock</strong> punishes any card you misplay or fail to use in time. Taken
    together, only a relatively low share of deals are winnable even with good play. That difficulty
    is the appeal: every game won is earned, and the planning rewards patience.
  </p>

  <h2>How you win</h2>
  <p>
    The game is won the moment all <strong>104 cards</strong> sit on the eight foundations — two
    complete suits in each colour, every one built cleanly from Ace to King. The tableau and the
    waste end up empty and the stock exhausted. Get there and you have beaten one of solitaire's
    sterner tests; if you don't, undo is there to back up and try a different line.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Now that you know the rules, <a href="/forty-thieves/">play Forty Thieves</a> and see how far
    you can get. When you want a gentler two-deck-strength challenge, try the open layout of
    <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a> or the spacious <a href="/eight-off/">Eight Off</a>, or browse
    everything in our <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
